Wed Nov  2 12:49:47 MST 2005
Trying to update this on each change from now on, not only on a new
release.

server -
	added better handling of the pid files /tmp/.dataman*.pid.
	if there were a termination of the processes without these
	being removed, the system wouldn't come back up.  also you
	can use the -q switch now to query the status of dataman.
client -
	better handling of termination of PHP.  db_discon is called
	on termination of PHP, but when called from a web page, there
	is no termination, so we are calling it at request end.  Then
	when it finally did happen we'd get a second call.  we want to
	ignore the second call.

Dataman 3.2.4
Fri Sep 16 14:25:46 MDT 2005
Overall, changed to autoconf 2.59 and automake1.9.3, included
some examples (in another cvs tree and package), and generally
tried to do some cleanup.  I know, there is a lot more cleanup
to do.
server -
	changed everything over to 64 bit offsets.  We really like
	to have big databases.  the make warns about using llseek,
	but i put the proper define in the header, and all seems to
	work for me.  also added mutex locks around seek/read combos
	so that shared locks on a file couldn't mess each other up.
	there was a bug in delete that sometimes would return the
	wrong current record.
client -
	of course changed these to handle the 64 bit offsets from the
	server.

Dataman 3.2.3
Sun Apr 24 19:03:00 MDT 2005
I haven't kept up the changelog for the last two
releases, so I thought I would get this one put
together.
server -
	added functions get_blobs and put_blobs.  purpose
	was to be able to add blobs to the data stream
	going back and forth between the server and clients.
	modified the utilities (mkdf, dumpdf, dfedit) that
	would allow for support for blobs.  also modified
	some of the file structures.
client -
	modified parsing routines, headers, and other routines
	to support blobs as well.  Also added the "-p" switch
	to init_dataman for support of the php module.  blob
	support includes c, c++, java, and the new php module.

7/7/04 - initial release.
